<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Transcribed by Divyash Maheshwari -->
<worksheet xml:id="se1i">
  <title>Linear Algebra Sample Exam 1I</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Work done on scratch paper will not be graded. You don't need to show your work on pages 1 and 2.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!-- Page 1 -->
<page>
  <exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (7 points) Indicate <term>true</term> if the statement is true, otherwise, indicate <term>false</term>.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col width="88%" />
        <col width="6%" />
        <col width="6%" />
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>true</cell>
            <cell>false</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>a) The transform <m>T(x_1, x_2) = (x_1, 1)</m> is a linear transform.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>b) If <m>A</m> is <m>m \times n</m>, <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, and there are some vectors <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m> that are not in the range of the transform <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, then there cannot be a pivot in every column of <m>A</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>c) If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and the homogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, then <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> must be consistent for every <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>d) If <m>\vec{v_3}</m> is not a linear combination of vectors <m>\vec{v_1} \text{ and } \vec{v_2}</m>, then the set <m>\{\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3}\}</m> is linearly independent.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>e) If the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is consistent and has a unique solution, then the vector <m>\vec{b}</m> cannot be the zero vector <m>\vec{0}</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>f) If the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is inconsistent, then the reduced row echelon form of the coefficient matrix <m>A</m> must contain a row of zeros.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>g) If two matrices have the same RREF, then they are row equivalent.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (5 points) Indicate whether the situations are <term>possible</term> or <term>impossible</term>.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col width="76%"/>
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="12%"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell><p>possible</p></cell>
            <cell><p>impossible</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>a) <m>A \text{ is a } 5 \times 4</m> matrix that has <m>2</m> pivot columns, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a linear system with <m>2</m> free variables.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>b) Vectors <m>\vec{x}\text{, } \vec{y}\text{, and } \vec{z}</m> are linearly dependent, but <m>\vec{x} \text{ and } \vec{y}</m> are linearly independent.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>c) Matrix <m>A \text{ is } m \times n\text{, } A\mathbf{x} = A\mathbf{y}</m>, vectors <m>\mathbf{x} \text{ and } \mathbf{y}</m> are in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, <m>\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y}</m>, and every row of <m>A</m> is pivotal.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>d) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a one-to-one linear transform and the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly dependent.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><p>e) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform, <m>\vec{x}</m> is a vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, <m>A \text{ is a } 2 \times 2</m> matrix with two pivots, and the only vector in the range of the transform is the zero vector.</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>

<!-- Page 2 -->
<page>
  <exercise workspace="0.1in">
    <introduction><p>(7 points) Fill in the blanks. You do not need to justify your reasoning.</p></introduction>
    <task>
      <statement><p>If <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> and <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform that first reflects points in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> through the line <m>x_1 = 0</m>, and then rotates them by <m>\pi / 2</m> radians clockwise about the origin, then <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 &amp; a_2 \\ a_3 &amp; a_4 \end{pmatrix}</m> where <m>a_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>a_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>a_3 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m> and <m>a_4 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Matrix <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m>, is in RREF, has exactly one pivot, and every entry of <m>A</m> is either <m>1 \text{ or } 0</m>. How many different matrices can you construct that meet all of these criteria? <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m></p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>If the linear mapping <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where <m>T_A \colon \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}^n</m>, is one-to-one and onto, then <m>n = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>A</m> has exactly <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m> pivots, the domain of <m>T_A \text{ is } \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, and the co-domain of <m>T_A \text{ is } \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, and the range of <m>T_A \text{ is } \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>If <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} k &amp; k \\ 4 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, then <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> is in the span of the columns of <m>A</m> when <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>. Also, <m>\vec{w} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ h \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to <m>A\vec{w} = \vec{0}</m> when <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{4em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Vector <m>b = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ k \end{pmatrix}</m> is in the plane spanned by <m>x = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>y = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m> when <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; h \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\vec{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ k \end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\vec{x} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}</m>. Then <m>\left( \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; h &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; k \end{array} \right)</m> is a representation of the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. The solution set of this system is the <m>z\text{-axis}</m> when <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m> and <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.</p></statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement><p>Suppose the entries of an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>B</m> are <m>b_{ij}</m>, and <m>n \gt 2</m>. If <m>b_{ij} = 0</m> when <m>i = j</m>, and <m>b_{ij} = 1</m> when <m>i \neq j</m>, how many pivot columns would <m>B</m> have? <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{1.4ex}}}</m></p></statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; -2 \\ 1 &amp; -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>B</m> is the standard matrix for a transform that rotates vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> clockwise by <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians, and <m>C</m> is the standard matrix for the transform <m>T_C \colon \vec{x} \mapsto C\vec{x}</m>, where <m>C =  BA</m>. In the grids below, sketch a) the range of the transform <m>\vec{x} \mapsto A\vec{x}</m>, and b) the range of the transform <m>\vec{x} \mapsto C\vec{x}</m>. You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
     <sidebyside widths="4% 32% 4% 32%">  
          <p> (a) </p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1i-4a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p> (b)</p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1i-4b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          </sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>

<!-- Page 3 -->
<page>
  <exercise workspace="0.25in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Write down the solutions to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> in parametric vector form. You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix} \text{, } \vec{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="4in">
    <statement>
      
      <p>
        (2 points) Please show your work for this question.
        </p><p>
        Construct a linear system and use row reductions to determine <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> so that the quadratic polynomial <m>{p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c}</m> passes through the points <m>P(-1, 0)</m>, <m>Q(0, 1)</m>, and <m>R(1, 4)</m>. Please show your work.
      </p>
      
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>